WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.357
Parallel reasoning matches the relationships between premises and conclusion.

2
00:00:05.657 --> 00:00:09.455
The topic can change while the logical structure stays the same.

3
00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:11.781
Every licensed courier is insured.

4
00:00:12.081 --> 00:00:13.681
This courier is licensed.

5
00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:15.816
Therefore this courier is insured.

6
00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:21.641
The argument supplies a rule and its sufficient condition, then draws the required result.

7
00:00:21.941 --> 00:00:25.483
A different argument about sealed jars and labels can match it.

8
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:33.079
An argument that starts from insurance and concludes licensing does not, even though it uses exactly the same topic.

9
00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:36.387
Matching vocabulary is not matching reasoning.

10
00:00:36.687 --> 00:00:45.433
Clear short labels let you compare structures without replacing complete ideas with opaque letters or overlooking what the conclusion claims.

11
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:49.509
Identify the rule, the given case and whether the inference follows.

12
00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:53.927
Retain all/most/some, negatives and joint conditions.

13
00:00:54.227 --> 00:00:57.427
Read the final claim as carefully as the first premise.

14
00:00:57.727 --> 00:01:05.129
A conditional conclusion is not a fact conclusion, and a joint sufficient condition is not two separate sufficient routes.

15
00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:08.309
The matching argument must keep those distinctions.
